\subsection{Encoder Ablation}
\label{sec:app_encoder}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Encoder} & \textbf{Dim} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{F1\textsubscript{stag}} & \textbf{F1\textsubscript{prog}} & \textbf{Best Ep.} \\
\midrule
Qwen3-0.6B & 1024 & 0.9055 & --- & --- & --- \\
E5-large-v2 & 1024 & 0.9363 & 0.9067 & 0.9660 & 4 \\
BGE-M3 & 1024 & \textbf{0.9413} & \textbf{0.9147} & \textbf{0.9679} & 5 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Encoder ablation on SWE-Agent conf${\geq}$2 ($N{=}1{,}824$). All models use the same GRU architecture (2-layer, hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1, lr$=$1e-3, batch$=$32, early stopping patience~5). All encoders exceed 0.90 macro-F1, confirming the probe's temporal architecture drives performance rather than a specific encoder. BGE-M3 achieves the highest score ($+$3.6\,pp over Qwen3), but the gap between encoders (0.5\,pp) is small relative to the structural--probe gap (8.96\,pp). ``---'' for Qwen3 indicates per-class scores were not logged in the initial sweep; macro-F1 is computed from the checkpoint with lowest validation loss.}
\label{tab:encoder_ablation}
\end{table}

